CAT = "sgm9aIntro"; SEG = "SPI"
Q = [
('A structured product typically combines a conventional investment with:',
 'A derivative', ['A second insurer', 'A bank overdraft', 'A trust deed only'],
 'A structured product pairs a deposit, bond or note with one or more derivatives, such as options or swaps, so its return follows a set formula linked to an underlying.'),
('The asset, index or rate that drives the payout of a structured product is its:',
 'Underlying', ['Coupon stream', 'Tenor', 'Insurance wrapper'],
 'The underlying (also called the reference asset) may be a share, a basket, an index, an interest rate, an exchange rate or a commodity. The payoff formula is applied to its performance.'),
('In a principal-protected note, the part that rebuilds the capital by maturity is usually:',
 'A zero-coupon bond', ['A bought call option', 'A currency swap', 'A share basket'],
 'The issuer buys a zero-coupon bond that grows to the capital amount at maturity, then spends the rest on options for the upside. The options give growth, not protection.'),
('A 5-year zero-coupon bond costs 82 per 100 of face value. Of 100 invested, how much is left to buy options?',
 '18', ['82', '100', '28'],
 'The bond at 82 grows to 100 by maturity, so 100 - 82 = 18 remains for the option component. Paying 82 for options would leave nothing to protect capital.'),
('When interest rates are lower at launch, a principal-protected product usually offers:',
 'Less upside participation', ['A higher guaranteed coupon', 'A shorter tenor by law', 'No issuer credit risk'],
 'Lower rates make the zero-coupon bond dearer, leaving less money for options, so the participation rate or cap falls. Credit risk on the issuer remains either way.'),
('A product pays 70% of the rise in an index over its term. The 70% is the:',
 'Participation rate', ['Barrier level', 'Strike price level', 'Knock-out rate'],
 'The participation rate is the share of the underlying gain passed to the investor. A barrier or knock-out level is a trigger price, not a share of the gain.'),
("Participation is 70% and the index rises 20% over the term. What is the investor's return?",
 '14%', ['20%', '13%', '7%'],
 'Return = 70% x 20% = 14%. Taking 70% of 10 points or ignoring the participation rate gives the wrong figures.'),
('Participation is 100%, capped at 15%. The index rises 26%. What does the product pay?',
 '15%', ['26%', '11%', '41%'],
 'The cap limits the return to 15% however far the index rises. The 11 points above the cap are given up in exchange for the protection or coupon.'),
('A maximum return written into a structured product is called a:',
 'Cap', ['Floor', 'Strike', 'Collar fee'],
 'A cap sets the highest return payable. A floor sets the lowest, often 0% or a small minimum, on principal-protected designs.'),
('A minimum return written into a structured product is called a:',
 'Floor', ['Cap', 'Barrier', 'Rebate'],
 'A floor guarantees that the return will not fall below a set level, often 0% at maturity. It is usually paid for by giving up some upside.'),
('A note pays a high coupon but may repay in shares if the share price falls below a set level. This is a:',
 'Reverse convertible', ['Range accrual note', 'Capital guaranteed fund', 'Zero-coupon bond'],
 'A reverse convertible pays an enhanced coupon because the investor has in effect sold a put: if the share falls far enough, it repays in shares worth less than the capital.'),
('A product that ends early and repays capital if the underlying is at or above a set level on an observation date is:',
 'An autocallable', ['A range accrual', 'A reverse floater', 'A dual currency deposit'],
 'An autocallable is redeemed automatically when the trigger is met on an observation date, usually with a fixed coupon. The investor then faces reinvestment risk.'),
('A note pays its coupon only for each day an interest rate stays inside a set band. This is a:',
 'Range accrual note', ['Reverse convertible', 'Credit-linked note', 'Plain floating rate note'],
 'A range accrual accrues the coupon only on days the reference rate or price is within the range, so the coupon actually paid can be well below the headline rate.'),
('A note whose capital is reduced if a named company suffers a default is a:',
 'Credit-linked note', ['Equity-linked note', 'Range accrual note', 'Callable deposit'],
 'MoneySense describes credit-linked notes as linked to a credit event such as insolvency or default of a specified entity. On such an event the investor can lose part or all of the capital.'),
('Equity-linked notes are tied to the performance of:',
 'Shares or share indices', ['Interest rate bands', 'Foreign currency pairs', "Named borrowers' default risk"],
 'Equity-linked notes reference a single share, a basket of shares, an equity index or a basket of indices. At repayment the investor may even receive shares instead of cash.'),
('A deposit that repays in a second currency if the exchange rate passes a strike is a:',
 'Dual currency investment', ['Range accrual deposit', 'Credit-linked deposit', 'Callable fixed deposit'],
 'A dual currency investment pays a higher interest rate, but the bank may repay principal in the alternative currency at the strike rate, which can be worth less.'),
('In a knock-in feature, the derivative becomes active only when the underlying:',
 'Hits the barrier', ['Pays a dividend', 'Reaches maturity', 'Changes its issuer'],
 'A knock-in option comes into existence once the barrier is touched. A knock-out works the other way: it ceases to exist when the barrier is touched.'),
('A knock-out option stops existing when the underlying:',
 'Reaches the barrier', ['Moves above the strike', 'Pays its first coupon', 'Is valued each month'],
 'Touching the knock-out barrier cancels the option, so any further gain from it is lost. Barrier options cost less than plain options for that reason.'),
('Compared with a fixed deposit, a structured deposit typically offers:',
 'Variable, linked returns', ['A fixed rate and tenor', 'Full deposit insurance', 'Same-day exit at par'],
 'MoneySense notes that structured deposits have variable returns and sometimes variable maturities, while fixed deposits have fixed returns and are covered by deposit insurance.'),
('A structured deposit held to maturity with a solvent bank should return:',
 'At least the principal', ['A guaranteed headline rate', 'The index gain in full', 'Principal plus inflation'],
 'MoneySense explains that a structured deposit held to maturity returns the full principal if the bank stays solvent, though the return itself can be zero.'),
('Under MAS guidelines, the label "structured deposit" may be used only for a product that is:',
 'Legally a deposit', ['Rated AA or better', 'Listed on SGX', 'Backed by a guarantee'],
 'FAA-G09 says a structured deposit is a type of deposit, and calling a product that is not a deposit a structured deposit is misleading.'),
('Which feature of a structured product most often appeals to a client who wants market exposure with limited downside?',
 'Protection at maturity', ['Daily liquidity at par', 'Unlimited upside', 'Full dividend income'],
 'A principal-protected structure limits the loss at maturity while giving some market-linked upside. It does not give daily liquidity at par or unlimited gains.'),
("Why does a structured note investor usually not receive the underlying shares' dividends?",
 'No share ownership', ['Dividends are taxed away', 'MAS bars dividend payouts', 'The trustee keeps them'],
 'The investor holds a debt obligation of the issuer, not the shares. Dividend value is typically used by the issuer to help price the embedded options.'),
('Fees on a structured note are often:',
 'Built into the pricing', ['Paid by MAS', 'Shown only after maturity', 'Charged to the trustee'],
 'Structuring and hedging costs are commonly embedded in the product terms rather than charged separately, so the investor should look for them in the prospectus.'),
('Who is legally obliged to make the payments on a structured note?',
 'The issuer', ['The distributor', 'The calculation agent', "The investor's adviser"],
 'A structured note is a debt obligation of its issuer. The distributor sells it and the calculation agent works out payouts, but neither owes the payments.'),
("The party that works out coupon and redemption amounts under a note's formula is the:",
 'Calculation agent', ['Arranger', 'Guarantor', 'Paying agent'],
 'The calculation agent applies the payoff formula and determines amounts due. Investors should note whether it is related to the issuer, as that is a potential conflict.'),
('A guarantor on a structured product adds protection only to the extent that:',
 'The guarantor stays solvent', ['The issuer has a higher rating', 'The product is listed on SGX', 'The tenor is under one year'],
 'A guarantee moves the credit risk to the guarantor. If the guarantor fails, the protection fails with it, which is why its rating must be disclosed.'),
("The short summary of a structured note's key features and risks given to retail investors is the:",
 'Product Highlights Sheet', ['Benefit illustration', 'Policy contract', 'Annual audited report'],
 'MoneySense says issuers should give a Product Highlights Sheet as well as the prospectus. The PHS summarises features and risks but does not replace the prospectus.'),
('For retail sale, a structured note offered to the public must come with a:',
 'Prospectus', ['Policy illustration', 'Trust certificate', 'Bank statement'],
 'Issuers offering structured notes to retail investors must provide a prospectus covering the offer, the issuer, risk factors and terms and conditions.'),
('Compared with owning the underlying shares, a principal-protected note usually gives:',
 'Less upside, less downside', ['More upside, more downside', 'The same return, no fees', 'Full dividends and votes'],
 'Protection at maturity and the cost of the options mean the investor usually gives up part of the gain and the dividends in exchange for limiting the loss.'),
('A structured product is most like a ready-made mix that a client could, in theory, build from:',
 'A bond and options', ['Two fixed deposits', 'Shares and a mortgage', 'A loan and an annuity'],
 'Most payoffs can be replicated with a bond or deposit plus long or short options. Packaging them gives retail access but adds fees and issuer risk.'),
('A tailor-made OTC structured product, compared with an exchange-traded one, usually has:',
 'Less liquidity', ['Lower issuer risk', 'Daily public prices', 'A central counterparty'],
 'OTC products are bespoke, so there is no exchange market or clearing house. The investor usually relies on the issuer for a buy-back price.'),
('Which client goal fits a capped, principal-protected product best?',
 'Some growth, capital kept', ['Maximum growth at any risk', 'Money needed next month', 'Regular dividend income'],
 'Such a product suits someone who can lock money away to maturity and accepts limited upside in exchange for getting capital back if the issuer is solvent.'),
('A bought call option inside a structured note gives the investor:',
 'Exposure to a rise', ['Protection on a fall', 'A fixed coupon', "The issuer's credit rating"],
 'Holding a call lets the note benefit if the underlying rises above the strike. Downside protection comes from the bond part or from a bought put.'),
('An enhanced coupon on a structured note is usually paid for by the investor:',
 'Accepting downside risk', ['Paying a higher fee upfront', 'Accepting a longer free-look', 'Lending shares to the issuer'],
 'A coupon above market rates is funded by the investor selling optionality, for example a put on shares, so losses can occur when the underlying falls.'),
('Which of these is a structured product wrapped in a life insurance policy?',
 'Structured ILP', ['Term rider', 'Fixed deposit', 'Government bond'],
 "A structured ILP is an investment-linked policy whose sub-fund invests in structured products, combining a defined payoff with the policy's insurance benefit."),
]
